<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Sumenep</h1><p class="st-meta">1269 CE – 1883 CE (approx.) · capital Sumenep</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of eastern Madura, whose first ruler Arya Wiraraja helped found Majapahit and whose princes ruled until 1883.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition dates Sumenep's founding to 1269, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara of Singhasari</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-wiraraja">Arya Wiraraja</a> as its adipati. Wiraraja is a well-known figure in Javanese chronicles: he advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> and then helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> defeat him and expel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1293, opening the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sumenep was a dry land of salt pans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and seafaring traders. Its rulers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 16th century and later accepted the overlordship of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a> and, from 1705, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-somala">Panembahan Somala</a> built the Great Mosque and the palace in the late 18th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdurrahman was a noted scholar of Javanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Sumenep's princes served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> as regents, supplying troops for colonial wars. In 1883 the colonial government abolished the royal title and ruled through ordinary regents. The palace and mosque remain landmarks of Madurese culture.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1269 CE – 1883 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.96%;width:12.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1269 CE – 1883 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sumenep</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arya Wiraraja, Panembahan Somala, Abdurrahman Pakunataningrat</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Madurese, Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sumenep</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sumenep" aria-label="Kingdom of Sumenep on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7832 2440 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Sumenep lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8152 2696L8052 2725M8152 2696L7959 2663M8152 2696L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8152 2696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8152 2696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sumenep. <a href="/atlas?year=1576&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sumenep">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
